  "intro": {
    "title": "Project Overview & Core Capabilities",
    "summary": "Meet nyairo: a place of their own for a digital individual to live, long-term memory stored locally on your machine, and an everyday life that moves with real time.",
    "toc": [
      {
        "id": "intro-section-0",
        "text": "Why build nyairo?"
      },
      {
        "id": "intro-section-1",
        "text": "What can it do today?"
      },
      {
        "id": "intro-section-2",
        "text": "What do you need before starting?"
      }
    ],
    "content": "<div class=\"callout callout-info\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Framework &amp; Persona Note</div><p>nyairo is an open-source framework for digital individuals (Apache-2.0). A fresh install ships with a clean, neutral baseline and no hardcoded character identity—you can name them, shape their tone, customize their room, and gradually grow a daily life together.</p></div>\n\n<h2 id=\"intro-section-0\">Why build nyairo?</h2>\n<p>With most AI chat tools, the moment you close the window, the other side's clock stops completely. The next time you open it, you are back to polite small talk from scratch. Saying you're going somewhere means instant teleportation, everything is forgotten as soon as the chat ends, and it rarely feels like someone real is living on the other side.</p>\n<p><strong>nyairo is built to solve that:</strong> they keep their own flow of time even after you close the window, have concrete rooms and streets to walk through, and keep your shared past safely stored on your own computer. Every conversation picks up right inside the life that kept unfolding since you last parted.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"intro-section-1\">What can it do today?</h2>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\">\n<table class=\"doc-table\">\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th scope=\"col\" style=\"width: 22%;\">Module</th>\n<th scope=\"col\" style=\"width: 50%;\">What It Does</th>\n<th scope=\"col\" style=\"width: 28%;\">Current Status</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Local Long-Term Memory &amp; Memory Lamp</strong></td>\n<td>Saves habits, promises, and shared experiences in a local SQLite database. A dual-layer memory lamp mechanism protects raw conversation transcripts from tampering; mistakes can be corrected face-to-face, and topics you would rather not revisit can be set to stop recalling.</td>\n<td>Enabled by default · Raw transcripts stay local</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Continuous Daily Life &amp; Healthy Boundaries</strong></td>\n<td>Location and active progress persist across window closes and reboots. Features non-servile, autonomous refusal boundaries (when they don't want to help, they decline plainly without canned customer-service apologies or holding grudges), asynchronous replies spaced hours apart on a real schedule, a phone reply inbox for non-urgent messages while sleeping, out, or focused, and the option to knock on their door for real emergencies.</td>\n<td>Core continuity on by default · Autonomous activity opt-in</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Neighborhood Shops &amp; Physical Item States</strong></td>\n<td>Outings are timed realistically along the street network, with 12 neighborhood shop types and open counters for free-form ordering (such as walking to the convenience store for hot oden). The used bookstore and coffee shop owners keep independent staggered schedules and support leaving coaster notes at the counter. Carry bags support up to 3 levels of nested storage, with realistic thermal and moisture item states—hot tea by a desk cools down naturally, and paper notes by a window turn slightly damp with blurred ink in the rain.</td>\n<td>Zero-setup randomized personal worlds + 7 built-in worlds + Light/Dark offline editor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Shared Reading, Manuscripts &amp; Moving Suitcase</strong></td>\n<td>Saves travel postcards and daily notes, with synchronized two-person reading annotations and manuscript revision diffs. The lossless moving suitcase (<code>.nyairo-suitcase.zip</code>) lets you pack everything up—including your world slice and a migration loss manifest.</td>\n<td>Available directly in the desktop app</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Standalone Analysis &amp; Advice</strong></td>\n<td>When facing a tough decision or debugging code, use a command to ask them to lay out trade-offs and ideas separately, then drop right back into everyday chat afterward.</td>\n<td>On demand (<code>/chiyo_consider</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n\n<h2 id=\"intro-section-2\">What do you need before starting?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>An ordinary computer</strong>: Windows 10 / 11 (via a ~6.0 MB admin-free desktop installer running with built-in WSL 2) or Linux. Conversation inference runs through the model endpoint you configure—<strong>no dedicated GPU required</strong>; an everyday thin-and-light laptop works fine.</li>\n<li><strong>An LLM API key (or free local Ollama)</strong>: Beginners can top up 5–10 CNY (~$1 USD) on the DeepSeek Open Platform (<code>platform.deepseek.com</code>) to create an <code>sk-...</code> key, or connect directly to a free local <code>Ollama</code> instance (<code>http://127.0.0.1:11434</code>) for zero cloud traffic.</li>\n<li><strong>A few minutes for setup</strong>: First-time installation automatically pulls the runtime environment and verification bundle—just keep your internet connection active.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Once you are ready, jump straight to <a href=\"#quickstart\">Getting Started: Your First Run</a>.</p>"
  },
